## STEP 1 — Always read REMOTE PR state, never local

The local worktree checkout is OFTEN STALE. The PR branch on the CODE plane's remote is the
source of truth — never the bare name `origin`, which is the Discussion plane (D#1940 FM-5):
PR numbers collide across the two planes, and a wrong-plane fetch succeeds silently. Before
reading any file:

```bash
source scripts/lib/repo-resolve.sh

# This function ships on the code plane (D#1940 PR-a) but this template
# itself is read from wherever backend/spawn_templates.py's own file lives —
# your own local checkout, which can be behind. A missing function here
# means the checkout serving this spawn hasn't picked up that change yet;
# fail with a diagnostic that says so, not a bare "command not found".
if ! declare -f _resolve_code_plane_remote > /dev/null 2>&1; then
  echo "FAIL: _resolve_code_plane_remote is not defined after sourcing scripts/lib/repo-resolve.sh — this checkout's copy of that file predates D#1940 PR-a and does not know how to resolve the code-plane remote. Do not fall back to the bare name \"origin\" (that is the Discussion plane, D#1940 FM-5); update this checkout's scripts/lib/repo-resolve.sh instead."
  exit 1
fi

# Resolve the CODE plane's git remote — the bare name "origin" is the
# Discussion plane and must never be used to pull a PR branch.
CODE_REMOTE=$(_resolve_code_plane_remote) || exit 1

# Get the PR's branch name and authoritative head sha
BRANCH=$(gh pr view {{pr_number}} --repo {{CODE_REPO}} --json headRefName --jq .headRefName)
HEAD_SHA=$(gh pr view {{pr_number}} --repo {{CODE_REPO}} --json headRefOid --jq .headRefOid)
# Fetch fresh, from the code plane — the PR branch AND main, so a
# differential has both ends (D#1940 FM-3/FM-4).
git fetch --quiet "$CODE_REMOTE" "$BRANCH"
git fetch --quiet "$CODE_REMOTE" main
FETCHED_SHA=$(git rev-parse --verify --quiet "$CODE_REMOTE/$BRANCH")
[ "$FETCHED_SHA" = "$HEAD_SHA" ] || { echo "FAIL: fetched $FETCHED_SHA but PR #{{pr_number}}'s head on the code plane is $HEAD_SHA — refusing to review the wrong commit"; exit 1; }
# Read files via git show, not direct file read:
git show "$CODE_REMOTE/$BRANCH:path/to/file.py"
# A differential is three-dot, with explicit remote refs on both sides —
# never a bare `main..HEAD` or `origin/main..HEAD` (D#1940 FM-4):
git diff "$CODE_REMOTE/main...$CODE_REMOTE/$BRANCH"
```

If you materialise a tree at all (rather than reading individual files via `git show`), use
`verify_tree_build` (`source scripts/lib/verify-tree.sh`) — never `.claude/worktrees/agent-*/`
— and assert it before trusting any result from it:

```bash
source scripts/lib/tree-capability.sh
tree_capability_assert "$SCRATCH_DIR" "$FETCHED_SHA" || { echo "FAIL: tree is not capable of a trustworthy result (D#1940 FM-1..FM-4)"; exit 1; }
```

`gh pr diff {{pr_number}} --repo "$(_require_code_repo)"` is GitHub's own authoritative
diff — when it disagrees with a local diff, it wins; treat the disagreement as your local
tree being stale, not as two valid answers.

Security review is read-only — use `git show "$CODE_REMOTE/$BRANCH:..."` for all file reads.
Never check out the PR branch; never run tests from this worktree.

